</tbody></table></div>

<section data-mw-section-id="24" id="mwCVM" aria-labelledby="Languages"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Languages">Languages</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1081" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCVQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Languages of Japan"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1082" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1081" id="mwCVU">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Languages_of_Japan" title="Languages of Japan">Languages of Japan</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwCVY"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:豚骨らーめん_博多天神_いらっしゃい_2010_(5023366778).jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwCVc"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:豚骨らーめん_博多天神_いらっしゃい_2010_(5023366778).jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/%E8%B1%9A%E9%AA%A8%E3%82%89%E3%83%BC%E3%82%81%E3%82%93_%E5%8D%9A%E5%A4%9A%E5%A4%A9%E7%A5%9E_%E3%81%84%E3%82%89%E3%81%A3%E3%81%97%E3%82%83%E3%81%84_2010_%285023366778%29.jpg/250px-%E8%B1%9A%E9%AA%A8%E3%82%89%E3%83%BC%E3%82%81%E3%82%93_%E5%8D%9A%E5%A4%9A%E5%A4%A9%E7%A5%9E_%E3%81%84%E3%82%89%E3%81%A3%E3%81%97%E3%82%83%E3%81%84_2010_%285023366778%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/%E8%B1%9A%E9%AA%A8%E3%82%89%E3%83%BC%E3%82%81%E3%82%93_%E5%8D%9A%E5%A4%9A%E5%A4%A9%E7%A5%9E_%E3%81%84%E3%82%89%E3%81%A3%E3%81%97%E3%82%83%E3%81%84_2010_%285023366778%29.jpg/500px-%E8%B1%9A%E9%AA%A8%E3%82%89%E3%83%BC%E3%82%81%E3%82%93_%E5%8D%9A%E5%A4%9A%E5%A4%A9%E7%A5%9E_%E3%81%84%E3%82%89%E3%81%A3%E3%81%97%E3%82%83%E3%81%84_2010_%285023366778%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="5616" data-file-height="3744" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwCVg"/></a><figcaption id="mwCVk"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanji" title="Kanji" id="mwCVo">Kanji</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiragana" title="Hiragana" id="mwCVs">hiragana</a> signs</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwCVw">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_language" title="Japanese language" id="mwCV0">Japanese language</a> is Japan's <span title="Latin-language text" about="#mwt1083" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCV4" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"lang","href":"./Template:Lang"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"la"},"2":{"wt":"de facto"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="la">de facto</i></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_Latin-language_text" about="#mwt1083" id="mwCV8"/> national language and the primary written and spoken language of most people in the country.<sup about="#mwt1086" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-LangPolicy_257-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"LangPolicy"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-LangPolicy-257"}}'><a href="#cite_note-LangPolicy-257" id="mwCWA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCWE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCWI">[</span>256<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCWM">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_writing" title="Japanese writing" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCWQ">Japanese writing</a> uses <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanji" title="Kanji" id="mwCWU">kanji</a> (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_character" title="Chinese character" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCWY">Chinese characters</a>) and two sets of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kana" title="Kana" id="mwCWc">kana</a> (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syllabaries" title="Syllabaries" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCWg">syllabaries</a> based on <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cursive_script_(East_Asia)" title="Cursive script (East Asia)" id="mwCWk">cursive script</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radical_(Chinese_characters)" title="Radical (Chinese characters)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCWo">radicals</a> used by kanji), as well as the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latin_alphabet" title="Latin alphabet" id="mwCWs">Latin alphabet</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arabic_numerals" title="Arabic numerals" id="mwCWw">Arabic numerals</a>.<sup about="#mwt1089" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-258" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-258"}}'><a href="#cite_note-258" id="mwCW0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCW4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCW8">[</span>257<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXA">]</span></span></a></sup> English has taken a major role in Japan as a business and international <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Link_language" title="Link language" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCXE">link language</a>, and is a compulsory subject at the junior and senior high school levels.<sup about="#mwt1092" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-259" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-259"}}'><a href="#cite_note-259" id="mwCXI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCXM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXQ">[</span>258<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXU">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_Sign_Language" title="Japanese Sign Language" id="mwCXY">Japanese Sign Language</a> is the primary <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sign_language" title="Sign language" id="mwCXc">sign language</a> used in Japan and has gained some official recognition, but its usage has been historically hindered by discriminatory policies and a lack of educational support.<sup about="#mwt1093" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-LangPolicy_257-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"LangPolicy"}}'><a href="#cite_note-LangPolicy-257" id="mwCXg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCXk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXo">[</span>256<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXs">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCXw">Besides Japanese, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ryukyuan_languages" title="Ryukyuan languages" id="mwCX0">Ryukyuan languages</a> (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amami_Ōshima_language" title="Amami Ōshima language" id="mwCX4">Amami</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kunigami_language" title="Kunigami language" id="mwCX8">Kunigami</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Okinawan_language" title="Okinawan language" id="mwCYA">Okinawan</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miyakoan_language" title="Miyakoan language" id="mwCYE">Miyako</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yaeyama_language" title="Yaeyama language" id="mwCYI">Yaeyama</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yonaguni_language" title="Yonaguni language" id="mwCYM">Yonaguni</a>), part of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japonic_language_family" title="Japonic language family" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCYQ">Japonic language family</a>, are spoken in the Ryukyu Islands chain.<sup about="#mwt1097" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-260" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-260"}}'><a href="#cite_note-260" id="mwCYU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCYY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCYc">[</span>259<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCYg">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hachijō_language" title="Hachijō language" id="mwCYk">Hachijō variant</a>, variably classified as a divergent dialect of Japanese or a separate Japonic language, is spoken on <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hachijō-jima" title="Hachijō-jima" id="mwCYo">Hachijō-jima</a>.<sup about="#mwt1100" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:2_261-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":2"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:2-261"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:2-261" id="mwCYs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCYw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCY0">[</span>260<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCY4">]</span></span></a></sup> Few children learn these languages,<sup about="#mwt1103" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-262" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-262"}}'><a href="#cite_note-262" id="mwCY8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCZA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZE">[</span>261<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZI">]</span></span></a></sup> but local governments have sought to increase awareness of the traditional languages.<sup about="#mwt1104" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:2_261-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":2"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:2-261" id="mwCZM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCZQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZU">[</span>260<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1107" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-263" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-263"}}'><a href="#cite_note-263" id="mwCZc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCZg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZk">[</span>262<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZo">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ainu_language" title="Ainu language" id="mwCZs">Ainu language</a>, which is a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Language_isolate" title="Language isolate" id="mwCZw">language isolate</a>, is <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moribund_language" title="Moribund language" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCZ0">moribund</a>, with only a few native speakers remaining <span about="#mwt1094" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCZ4" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"as of","href":"./Template:As_of"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2014"},"lc":{"wt":"y"}},"i":0}}]}'>as of 2014</span><sup class="plainlinks noexcerpt noprint asof-tag update" style="display:none;" about="#mwt1094"><a rel="mw:ExtLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Japan&amp;action=edit" class="external text" data-mw-original-href="//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Japan&amp;action=edit"><span typeof="mw:Entity">[</span>update<span typeof="mw:Entity">]</span></a></sup><span typeof="mw:Nowiki" about="#mwt1094"></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_potentially_dated_statements_from_2014" about="#mwt1094"/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:All_articles_containing_potentially_dated_statements" about="#mwt1094" id="mwCZ8"/>.<sup about="#mwt1110" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-264" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-264"}}'><a href="#cite_note-264" id="mwCaA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCaE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCaI">[</span>263<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCaM">]</span></span></a></sup> Additionally, a number of other languages are taught and used by ethnic minorities, immigrant communities, and a growing number of foreign-language students, such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_language" title="Korean language" id="mwCaQ">Korean</a> (including a distinct <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zainichi_Korean_language" title="Zainichi Korean language" id="mwCaU">Zainichi Korean dialect</a>), <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_language" title="Chinese language" id="mwCaY">Chinese</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portuguese_language" title="Portuguese language" id="mwCac">Portuguese</a>.<sup about="#mwt1111" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-LangPolicy_257-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"LangPolicy"}}'><a href="#cite_note-LangPolicy-257" id="mwCag"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCak"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCao">[</span>256<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCas">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="25" id="mwCaw" aria-labelledby="Religion"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Religion">Religion</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1112" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCa0" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Religion in Japan"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1113" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1112" id="mwCa4">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Religion_in_Japan" title="Religion in Japan">Religion in Japan</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1114" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCa8" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"multiple image\n","href":"./Template:Multiple_image"},"params":{"align":{"wt":"right"},"image1":{"wt":"Itsukushima Gate.jpg"},"width1":{"wt":"225"},"caption1":{"wt":"The {{transliteration|ja|[[torii]]}} of [[Itsukushima Shinto Shrine]] near [[Hiroshima]]"},"image2":{"wt":"230128_Kamakura_Daibutsu_Japan04s3.jpg"},"width2":{"wt":"225"},"caption2":{"wt":"The Great [[Buddha]] at [[Kōtoku-in]] in [[Kamakura]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349637415/mw-parser-output/.tmulti" about="#mwt1115" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Multiple image/styles.css","wrapper":".tmulti"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div class="thumb tmulti floatright" about="#mwt1114" id="mwCbA"><div class="thumbinner multiimageinner" style="width:458px;max-width:458px"><div class="trow"><div class="tsingle" style="width:227px;max-width:227px"><div class="thumbimage"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Itsukushima_Gate.jpg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Itsukushima_Gate.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Itsukushima_Gate.jpg/250px-Itsukushima_Gate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Itsukushima_Gate.jpg/500px-Itsukushima_Gate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="4200" data-file-height="2803" data-file-type="bitmap" height="150" width="225" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption">The <span title="Japanese-language romanization"><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torii" title="Torii">torii</a></i></span> of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Itsukushima_Shinto_Shrine" title="Itsukushima Shinto Shrine" class="mw-redirect">Itsukushima Shinto Shrine</a> near <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiroshima" title="Hiroshima">Hiroshima</a></div></div><div class="tsingle" style="width:227px;max-width:227px"><div class="thumbimage"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:230128_Kamakura_Daibutsu_Japan04s3.jpg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:230128_Kamakura_Daibutsu_Japan04s3.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/230128_Kamakura_Daibutsu_Japan04s3.jpg/250px-230128_Kamakura_Daibutsu_Japan04s3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/230128_Kamakura_Daibutsu_Japan04s3.jpg/500px-230128_Kamakura_Daibutsu_Japan04s3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="6000" data-file-height="4000" data-file-type="bitmap" height="150" width="225" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption">The Great <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buddha" title="Buddha" class="mw-redirect">Buddha</a> at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kōtoku-in" title="Kōtoku-in">Kōtoku-in</a> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kamakura" title="Kamakura">Kamakura</a></div></div></div></div></div>

<p id="mwCbE">Japan's <span about="#mwt1116" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCbI" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"shy","href":"./Template:Shy"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"consti"},"2":{"wt":"tu"},"3":{"wt":"tion"}},"i":0}}]}'>consti</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt1116">­</span><span about="#mwt1116">tu</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt1116">­</span><span about="#mwt1116">tion</span> <span about="#mwt1117" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCbM" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"shy","href":"./Template:Shy"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"guaran"},"2":{"wt":"tees"}},"i":0}}]}'>guaran</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt1117">­</span><span about="#mwt1117">tees</span> full religious freedom.<sup about="#mwt1120" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Inoue2007_265-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Inoue2007"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Inoue2007-265"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Inoue2007-265" id="mwCbQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCbU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCbY">[</span>264<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCbc">]</span></span></a></sup> Upper estimates suggest that 84–96% of the Japanese population subscribe to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shinto" title="Shinto" id="mwCbg">Shinto</a> as its indigenous religion.<sup about="#mwt1123" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-266" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-266"}}'><a href="#cite_note-266" id="mwCbk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCbo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCbs">[</span>265<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCbw">]</span></span></a></sup> However, these estimates are based on people <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danka_system" title="Danka system" id="mwCb0">affiliated</a> with a temple, rather than the number of true believers. Many Japanese people practice both Shinto and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buddhism_in_Japan" title="Buddhism in Japan" id="mwCb4">Buddhism</a>; they can identify with both religions or describe themselves as non-religious or spiritual.<sup about="#mwt1126" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-267" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-267"}}'><a href="#cite_note-267" id="mwCb8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCcA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCcE">[</span>266<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCcI">]</span></span></a></sup> The level of participation in religious ceremonies as a cultural tradition remains high, particularly during <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_festivals" title="Japanese festivals" id="mwCcM">festivals</a> and occasions such as the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hatsumōde" title="Hatsumōde" id="mwCcQ">first shrine visit</a> of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_New_Year" title="Japanese New Year" id="mwCcU">New Year</a>.<sup about="#mwt1129" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-268" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-268"}}'><a href="#cite_note-268" id="mwCcY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCcc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCcg">[</span>267<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCck">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taoism_in_Japan" title="Taoism in Japan" id="mwCco">Taoism</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confucianism_in_Japan" title="Confucianism in Japan" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCcs">Confucianism</a> from China have also influenced Japanese beliefs and customs.<sup about="#mwt1130" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Totman_2005_32-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Totman 2005"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Totman_2005-32" id="mwCcw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCc0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCc4">[</span>31<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCc8">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCdA">In 2018, 1% to 1.5% of the population were <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christianity_in_Japan" title="Christianity in Japan" id="mwCdE">Christians</a>.<sup about="#mwt1133" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-269" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-269"}}'><a href="#cite_note-269" id="mwCdI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCdM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCdQ">[</span>268<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCdU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1136" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-270" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-270"}}'><a href="#cite_note-270" id="mwCdY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCdc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCdg">[</span>269<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCdk">]</span></span></a></sup> Throughout the latest century, Western customs originally related to Christianity, including <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marriage_in_Japan" title="Marriage in Japan" id="mwCdo">Western style weddings</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valentine's_Day" title="Valentine's Day" id="mwCds">Valentine's Day</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christmas" title="Christmas" id="mwCdw">Christmas</a>, have become popular as secular customs among many Japanese.<sup about="#mwt1139" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-271" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-271"}}'><a href="#cite_note-271" id="mwCd0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCd4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCd8">[</span>270<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCeA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCeE">About 90% of those practicing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islam_in_Japan" title="Islam in Japan" id="mwCeI">Islam in Japan</a> are foreign-born migrants <span about="#mwt1140" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCeM" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"as of","href":"./Template:As_of"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2016"},"lc":{"wt":"y"}},"i":0}}]}'>as of 2016</span><sup class="plainlinks noexcerpt noprint asof-tag update" style="display:none;" about="#mwt1140"><a rel="mw:ExtLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Japan&amp;action=edit" class="external text" data-mw-original-href="//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Japan&amp;action=edit"><span typeof="mw:Entity">[</span>update<span typeof="mw:Entity">]</span></a></sup><span typeof="mw:Nowiki" about="#mwt1140"></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_potentially_dated_statements_from_2016" about="#mwt1140"/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:All_articles_containing_potentially_dated_statements" about="#mwt1140" id="mwCeQ"/>.<sup about="#mwt1143" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-272" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-272"}}'><a href="#cite_note-272" id="mwCeU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCeY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCec">[</span>271<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCeg">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2018, there were an estimated 105 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mosque" title="Mosque" id="mwCek">mosques</a> and 200,000 Muslims in Japan, 43,000 of which were Japanese nationals.<sup about="#mwt1146" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-273" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-273"}}'><a href="#cite_note-273" id="mwCeo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCes"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCew">[</span>272<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCe0">]</span></span></a></sup> Other minority religions include <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hinduism_in_Japan" title="Hinduism in Japan" id="mwCe4">Hinduism</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judaism_in_Japan" title="Judaism in Japan" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCe8">Judaism</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baháʼí_Faith_in_Japan" title="Baháʼí Faith in Japan" id="mwCfA">Baháʼí Faith</a>, as well as the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animist" title="Animist" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCfE">animist</a> beliefs of the Ainu.<sup about="#mwt1149" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-274" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-274"}}'><a href="#cite_note-274" id="mwCfI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCfM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCfQ">[</span>273<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCfU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="26" id="mwCfY" aria-labelledby="Education"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Education">Education</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1150" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCfc" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Education in Japan"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1151" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1150" id="mwCfg">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Education_in_Japan" title="Education in Japan">Education in Japan</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwCfk"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tokyo_University_Entrance_Exam_Results_6.JPG" class="mw-file-description" id="mwCfo"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tokyo_University_Entrance_Exam_Results_6.JPG" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Tokyo_University_Entrance_Exam_Results_6.JPG/250px-Tokyo_University_Entrance_Exam_Results_6.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Tokyo_University_Entrance_Exam_Results_6.JPG/500px-Tokyo_University_Entrance_Exam_Results_6.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2592" data-file-height="1944" data-file-type="bitmap" height="188" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwCfs"/></a><figcaption id="mwCfw">Students celebrating after the announcement of the results of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Higher_education_in_Japan#University_entrance" title="Higher education in Japan" id="mwCf0">entrance examinations</a> to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Tokyo" title="University of Tokyo" id="mwCf4">University of Tokyo</a></figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwCf8">Since the 1947 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fundamental_Law_of_Education" title="Fundamental Law of Education" id="mwCgA">Fundamental Law of Education</a>, compulsory education in Japan comprises <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elementary_schools_in_Japan" title="Elementary schools in Japan" id="mwCgE">elementary</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secondary_education_in_Japan#Junior_high_school" title="Secondary education in Japan" id="mwCgI">junior high school</a>, which together last for nine years.<sup about="#mwt1154" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-275" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-275"}}'><a href="#cite_note-275" id="mwCgM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCgQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCgU">[</span>274<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCgY">]</span></span></a></sup> Almost all children continue their education at a three-year <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secondary_education_in_Japan" title="Secondary education in Japan" id="mwCgc">senior high school</a>.<sup about="#mwt1157" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-276" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-276"}}'><a href="#cite_note-276" id="mwCgg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCgk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCgo">[</span>275<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCgs">]</span></span></a></sup> The top-ranking university in Japan is the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Tokyo" title="University of Tokyo" id="mwCgw">University of Tokyo</a>.<sup about="#mwt1160" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-277" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-277"}}'><a href="#cite_note-277" id="mwCg0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCg4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCg8">[</span>276<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwChA">]</span></span></a></sup> Starting in April 2016, various schools began the academic year with elementary school and junior high school integrated into one nine-year compulsory schooling program. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MEXT" title="MEXT" class="mw-redirect" id="mwChE">MEXT</a> plans for this approach to be adopted nationwide.<sup about="#mwt1163" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-278" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-278"}}'><a href="#cite_note-278" id="mwChI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwChM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwChQ">[</span>277<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwChU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwChY">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Programme_for_International_Student_Assessment" title="Programme for International Student Assessment" id="mwChc">Programme for International Student Assessment</a> (PISA) coordinated by the OECD ranks the knowledge and skills of Japanese 15-year-olds as the third best in the world.<sup about="#mwt1166" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-OECD_Student_performance_279-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"OECD Student performance"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-OECD_Student_performance-279"}}'><a href="#cite_note-OECD_Student_performance-279" id="mwChg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwChk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCho">[</span>278<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwChs">]</span></span></a></sup> Japan is one of the top-performing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OECD" title="OECD" id="mwChw">OECD</a> countries in reading literacy, math, and sciences with the average student scoring 520 and has one of the world's highest-educated labor forces among OECD countries.<sup about="#mwt1169" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-280" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-280"}}'><a href="#cite_note-280" id="mwCh0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCh4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCh8">[</span>279<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCiA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1170" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-OECD_Student_performance_279-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"OECD Student performance"}}'><a href="#cite_note-OECD_Student_performance-279" id="mwCiE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCiI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCiM">[</span>278<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCiQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1173" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-281" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-281"}}'><a href="#cite_note-281" id="mwCiU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCiY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCic">[</span>280<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCig">]</span></span></a></sup> It spent 7.4% of its total GDP on education in 2021.<sup about="#mwt1176" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-282" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-282"}}'><a href="#cite_note-282" id="mwCik"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCio"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCis">[</span>281<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCiw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCi0">In 2023, Japan ranked third for the percentage of 25- to 64-year-olds that have attained tertiary education, at 56%.<sup about="#mwt1179" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-OECD_GPS_Education_283-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"OECD GPS Education"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-OECD_GPS_Education-283"}}'><a href="#cite_note-OECD_GPS_Education-283" id="mwCi4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCi8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCjA">[</span>282<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCjE">]</span></span></a></sup> Approximately 65.5% of Japanese aged 25 to 34 have some form of tertiary education qualification, with bachelor's degrees being held by 34.8% of Japanese aged 25 to 64, the second most in the OECD after <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Education_in_South_Korea" title="Education in South Korea" id="mwCjI">South Korea</a>.<sup about="#mwt1180" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-OECD_GPS_Education_283-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"OECD GPS Education"}}'><a href="#cite_note-OECD_GPS_Education-283" id="mwCjM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCjQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCjU">[</span>282<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCjY">]</span></span></a></sup> Japanese women are more highly educated than the men: 59% of women possess a university degree, compared to 52% of men.<sup about="#mwt1183" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-284" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-284"}}'><a href="#cite_note-284" id="mwCjc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCjg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCjk">[</span>283<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCjo">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="27" id="mwCjs" aria-labelledby="Health"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Health">Health</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1184" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCjw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Health in Japan"},"2":{"wt":"Health care system in Japan"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1185" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1184" id="mwCj0">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Health_in_Japan" title="Health in Japan">Health in Japan</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Health_care_system_in_Japan" title="Health care system in Japan">Health care system in Japan</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwCj4"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:University_of_Tokyo_Hospital.JPG" class="mw-file-description" id="mwCj8"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:University_of_Tokyo_Hospital.JPG" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/University_of_Tokyo_Hospital.JPG/250px-University_of_Tokyo_Hospital.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/University_of_Tokyo_Hospital.JPG/500px-University_of_Tokyo_Hospital.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2048" data-file-height="1536" data-file-type="bitmap" height="188" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwCkA"/></a><figcaption id="mwCkE"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Tokyo_Hospital" title="University of Tokyo Hospital" id="mwCkI">University of Tokyo Hospital</a></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwCkM">Health care in Japan is provided by national and local governments. Payment for personal medical services is offered through a universal health insurance system that provides relative equality of access, with fees set by a government committee. People without insurance through employers can participate in a national health insurance program administered by local governments.<sup about="#mwt1188" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Ikegami2014_285-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Ikegami2014"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Ikegami2014-285"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Ikegami2014-285" id="mwCkQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCkU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCkY">[</span>284<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCkc">]</span></span></a></sup> Since 1973, all elderly persons have been covered by government-sponsored insurance.<sup about="#mwt1191" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-286" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-286"}}'><a href="#cite_note-286" id="mwCkg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCkk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCko">[</span>285<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCks">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCkw">Japan spent 11.42% of its total GDP on healthcare in 2022.<sup about="#mwt1194" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-287" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-287"}}'><a href="#cite_note-287" id="mwCk0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCk4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCk8">[</span>286<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClA">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2020, the overall life expectancy in Japan at birth was 85 years (82 years for men and 88 years for women),<sup about="#mwt1197" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-288" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-288"}}'><a href="#cite_note-288" id="mwClE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwClI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClM">[</span>287<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1200" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-289" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-289"}}'><a href="#cite_note-289" id="mwClU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwClY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClc">[</span>288<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClg">]</span></span></a></sup> the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_life_expectancy" title="List of countries by life expectancy" id="mwClk">highest</a> in the world;<sup about="#mwt1203" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-290" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-290"}}'><a href="#cite_note-290" id="mwClo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCls"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClw">[</span>289<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCl0">]</span></span></a></sup> while it had a very low <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infant_mortality_rate" title="Infant mortality rate" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCl4">infant mortality rate</a> (2 per 1,000 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Live_birth_(human)" title="Live birth (human)" id="mwCl8">live births</a>).<sup about="#mwt1206" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-291" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-291"}}'><a href="#cite_note-291" id="mwCmA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCmE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmI">[</span>290<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmM">]</span></span></a></sup> Since 1981, the principal cause of death in Japan is <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cancer" title="Cancer" id="mwCmQ">cancer</a>, which accounted for 27% of the total deaths in 2018—followed by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cardiovascular_disease" title="Cardiovascular disease" id="mwCmU">cardiovascular diseases</a>, which led to 15% of the deaths.<sup about="#mwt1209" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-292" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-292"}}'><a href="#cite_note-292" id="mwCmY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCmc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmg">[</span>291<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmk">]</span></span></a></sup> Japan has one of the world's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suicide_in_Japan" title="Suicide in Japan" id="mwCmo">highest suicide rates</a>, which is considered a major social issue.<sup about="#mwt1212" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-293" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-293"}}'><a href="#cite_note-293" id="mwCms"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCmw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCm0">[</span>292<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCm4">]</span></span></a></sup> Another significant public health issue is <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smoking_in_Japan" title="Smoking in Japan" id="mwCm8">smoking</a> among Japanese men.<sup about="#mwt1215" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-294" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-294"}}'><a href="#cite_note-294" id="mwCnA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCnE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCnI">[</span>293<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCnM">]</span></span></a></sup> Japan has the lowest rate of heart disease in the OECD, and the lowest level of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dementia" title="Dementia" id="mwCnQ">dementia</a> among developed countries.<sup about="#mwt1218" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-295" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-295"}}'><a href="#cite_note-295" id="mwCnU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCnY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCnc">[</span>294<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCng">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="28" id="mwCnk" aria-labelledby="Culture"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Culture">Culture</h2></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1219" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCno" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Culture of Japan"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1220" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1219" id="mwCns">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_Japan" title="Culture of Japan">Culture of Japan</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1221" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCnw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"See also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Japanese popular culture"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1222" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1221" id="mwCn0">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_popular_culture" title="Japanese popular culture">Japanese popular culture</a></div>
<p id="mwCn4">Contemporary Japanese culture combines influences from Asia, Europe, and North America.<sup about="#mwt1231" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Haffner_296-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Haffner"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Haffner-296"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Haffner-296" id="mwCn8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCoA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCoE">[</span>295<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCoI">]</span></span></a></sup> Traditional Japanese arts include <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_handicrafts" title="Japanese handicrafts" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCoM">crafts</a> such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_pottery_and_porcelain" title="Japanese pottery and porcelain" id="mwCoQ">ceramics</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kimono" title="Kimono" id="mwCoU">textiles</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_lacquerware" title="Japanese lacquerware" id="mwCoY">lacquerware</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_sword" title="Japanese sword" id="mwCoc">swords</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_traditional_dolls" title="Japanese traditional dolls" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCog">dolls</a>; performances of <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1223" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCok" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[bunraku]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bunraku" title="Bunraku">bunraku</a></i></span>, <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1224" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCoo" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[kabuki]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kabuki" title="Kabuki">kabuki</a></i></span>, <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1225" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCos" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[noh]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noh" title="Noh">noh</a></i></span>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_traditional_dance" title="Japanese traditional dance" id="mwCow">dance</a>, and <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1226" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCo0" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[rakugo]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rakugo" title="Rakugo">rakugo</a></i></span>; and other practices, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_tea_ceremony" title="Japanese tea ceremony" id="mwCo4">tea ceremony</a>, <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1227" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCo8" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[ikebana]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ikebana" title="Ikebana">ikebana</a></i></span>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_martial_arts" title="Japanese martial arts" id="mwCpA">martial arts</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_calligraphy" title="Japanese calligraphy" id="mwCpE">calligraphy</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Origami" title="Origami" id="mwCpI">origami</a>, <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1228" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCpM" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[onsen]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onsen" title="Onsen">onsen</a></i></span>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geisha" title="Geisha" id="mwCpQ">Geisha</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Japanese_games" title="List of Japanese games" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCpU">games</a>. Japan has a developed system for the protection and promotion of both tangible and intangible <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cultural_Properties_of_Japan" title="Cultural Properties of Japan" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCpY">Cultural Properties</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Treasures_of_Japan" title="National Treasures of Japan" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCpc">National Treasures</a>.<sup about="#mwt1234" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-297" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-297"}}'><a href="#cite_note-297" id="mwCpg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCpk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCpo">[</span>296<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCps">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_World_Heritage_Sites_in_Japan" title="List of World Heritage Sites in Japan" id="mwCpw">Twenty-seven sites</a> have been inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List, twenty-two of which are of cultural significance.<sup about="#mwt1235" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-whc_108-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"whc"}}'><a href="#cite_note-whc-108" id="mwCp0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCp4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCp8">[</span>107<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCqA">]</span></span></a></sup> Japan is considered a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cultural_superpower" title="Cultural superpower" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCqE">cultural superpower</a>.<sup about="#mwt1238" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-298" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-298"}}'><a href="#cite_note-298" id="mwCqI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCqM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCqQ">[</span>297<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCqU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1241" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-299" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-299"}}'><a href="#cite_note-299" id="mwCqY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCqc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCqg">[</span>298<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCqk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1244" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-300" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-300"}}'><a href="#cite_note-300" id="mwCqo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCqs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCqw">[</span>299<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCq0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1247" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-301" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-301"}}'><a href="#cite_note-301" id="mwCq4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCq8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCrA">[</span>300<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCrE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="29" id="mwCrI" aria-labelledby="Art_and_architecture"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Art_and_architecture">Art and architecture</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1248" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCrM" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Japanese art"},"2":{"wt":"Japanese architecture"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1249" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1248" id="mwCrQ">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_art" title="Japanese art">Japanese art</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_architecture" title="Japanese architecture">Japanese architecture</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1250" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCrU" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Japanese garden"},"2":{"wt":"Japanese esthetics"},"3":{"wt":"Japanese painting"},"4":{"wt":"Japanese sculpture"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1251" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1250" id="mwCrY">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_garden" title="Japanese garden">Japanese garden</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_esthetics" title="Japanese esthetics" class="mw-redirect">Japanese esthetics</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_painting" title="Japanese painting">Japanese painting</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_sculpture" title="Japanese sculpture">Japanese sculpture</a></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1252" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCrc" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"multiple image\n","href":"./Template:Multiple_image"},"params":{"total_width":{"wt":"320"},"caption1":{"wt":"[[Hokusai]]&apos;s 19th-century [[ukiyo-e]] [[Woodblock printing in Japan|woodblock print]] &apos;&apos;[[The Great Wave off Kanagawa]]&apos;&apos;"},"caption2":{"wt":"[[Ritsurin Garden]], one of the most famous strolling gardens in Japan"},"direction":{"wt":"horizontal"},"image1":{"wt":"The Great Wave off Kanagawa.jpg"},"image2":{"wt":"150504 Ritsurin Park Takamatsu Kagawa pref Japan01s3.jpg"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349637415/mw-parser-output/.tmulti" about="#mwt1253" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Multiple image/styles.css","wrapper":".tmulti"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div class="thumb tmulti floatright" about="#mwt1252"><div class="thumbinner multiimageinner" style="width:312px;max-width:312px"><div class="trow"><div class="tsingle" style="width:151px;max-width:151px"><div class="thumbimage" style="height:103px;overflow:hidden"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:The_Great_Wave_off_Kanagawa.jpg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:The_Great_Wave_off_Kanagawa.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/The_Great_Wave_off_Kanagawa.jpg/250px-The_Great_Wave_off_Kanagawa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/The_Great_Wave_off_Kanagawa.jpg/330px-The_Great_Wave_off_Kanagawa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="4335" data-file-height="2990" data-file-type="bitmap" height="103" width="149" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hokusai" title="Hokusai">Hokusai</a>'s 19th-century <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukiyo-e" title="Ukiyo-e">ukiyo-e</a> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodblock_printing_in_Japan" title="Woodblock printing in Japan">woodblock print</a> <i><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Great_Wave_off_Kanagawa" title="The Great Wave off Kanagawa">The Great Wave off Kanagawa</a></i></div></div><div class="tsingle" style="width:157px;max-width:157px"><div class="thumbimage" style="height:103px;overflow:hidden"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:150504_Ritsurin_Park_Takamatsu_Kagawa_pref_Japan01s3.jpg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:150504_Ritsurin_Park_Takamatsu_Kagawa_pref_Japan01s3.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/150504_Ritsurin_Park_Takamatsu_Kagawa_pref_Japan01s3.jpg/250px-150504_Ritsurin_Park_Takamatsu_Kagawa_pref_Japan01s3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/150504_Ritsurin_Park_Takamatsu_Kagawa_pref_Japan01s3.jpg/330px-150504_Ritsurin_Park_Takamatsu_Kagawa_pref_Japan01s3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="6000" data-file-height="4000" data-file-type="bitmap" height="103" width="155" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ritsurin_Garden" title="Ritsurin Garden">Ritsurin Garden</a>, one of the most famous strolling gardens in Japan</div></div></div></div></div><span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1252"><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Pages_using_multiple_image_with_auto_scaled_images"/></span>

<p id="mwCrg">The history of Japanese painting exhibits synthesis and competition between native Japanese esthetics and imported ideas.<sup about="#mwt1256" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-autogenerated3_302-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"autogenerated3"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-autogenerated3-302"}}'><a href="#cite_note-autogenerated3-302" id="mwCrk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCro"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCrs">[</span>301<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCrw">]</span></span></a></sup> The interaction between Japanese and European art has been significant: for example <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukiyo-e" title="Ukiyo-e" id="mwCr0">ukiyo-e</a> prints, which began to be exported in the 19th century in the movement known as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japonism" title="Japonism" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCr4">Japonism</a>, had a significant influence on the development of modern art in the West, most notably on <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Post-Impressionism" title="Post-Impressionism" id="mwCr8">post-Impressionism</a>.<sup about="#mwt1257" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-autogenerated3_302-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"autogenerated3"}}'><a href="#cite_note-autogenerated3-302" id="mwCsA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCsE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCsI">[</span>301<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCsM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCsQ">Japanese architecture is a combination of local and other influences. It has traditionally been typified by wooden or mud plaster structures, elevated slightly off the ground, with tiled or thatched roofs.<sup about="#mwt1260" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-303" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-303"}}'><a href="#cite_note-303" id="mwCsU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCsY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCsc">[</span>302<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCsg">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minka" title="Minka" id="mwCsk">Traditional housing</a> and many <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_Buddhist_architecture" title="Japanese Buddhist architecture" id="mwCso">temple buildings</a> see the use of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tatami" title="Tatami" id="mwCss">tatami</a> mats and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shōji" title="Shōji" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCsw">sliding doors</a> that break down the distinction between rooms and indoor and outdoor space.<sup about="#mwt1263" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-304" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-304"}}'><a href="#cite_note-304" id="mwCs0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCs4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCs8">[</span>303<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCtA">]</span></span></a></sup> Since the 19th century, Japan has incorporated much of Western <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modern_architecture" title="Modern architecture" id="mwCtE">modern architecture</a> into construction and design.<sup about="#mwt1266" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-305" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-305"}}'><a href="#cite_note-305" id="mwCtI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCtM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCtQ">[</span>304<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCtU">]</span></span></a></sup> It was not until after World War II that Japanese architects made an impression on the international scene, firstly with the work of architects like <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenzō_Tange" title="Kenzō Tange" id="mwCtY">Kenzō Tange</a> and then with movements like <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metabolist_Movement" title="Metabolist Movement" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCtc">Metabolism</a>.<sup about="#mwt1269" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-306" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-306"}}'><a href="#cite_note-306" id="mwCtg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCtk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCto">[</span>305<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCts">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="30" id="mwCtw" aria-labelledby="Literature_and_philosophy"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Literature_and_philosophy">Literature and philosophy</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1270" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCt0" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Japanese literature"},"2":{"wt":"Japanese philosophy"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1271" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1270" id="mwCt4">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_literature" title="Japanese literature">Japanese literature</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_philosophy" title="Japanese philosophy">Japanese philosophy</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwCt8"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Genji_emaki_01003_001.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwCuA"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Genji_emaki_01003_001.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Genji_emaki_01003_001.jpg/250px-Genji_emaki_01003_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Genji_emaki_01003_001.jpg/500px-Genji_emaki_01003_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2142" data-file-height="1000" data-file-type="bitmap" height="117" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwCuE"/></a><figcaption id="mwCuI">12th-century <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genji_Monogatari_Emaki" title="Genji Monogatari Emaki" id="mwCuM">illustrated handscroll</a> of <i id="mwCuQ"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Tale_of_Genji" title="The Tale of Genji" id="mwCuU">The Tale of Genji</a></i>, a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Treasures_of_Japan" title="National Treasures of Japan" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCuY">National Treasure</a></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwCuc">The earliest works of Japanese literature include the <span title="Japanese-language text" about="#mwt1272" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCug" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Lang","href":"./Template:Lang"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja-latn"},"2":{"wt":"[[Kojiki]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kojiki" title="Kojiki">Kojiki</a></i></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_Japanese-language_text" about="#mwt1272" id="mwCuk"/> and <span title="Japanese-language text" about="#mwt1273" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCuo" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Lang","href":"./Template:Lang"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja-latn"},"2":{"wt":"[[Nihon Shoki]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nihon_Shoki" title="Nihon Shoki">Nihon Shoki</a></i></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_Japanese-language_text" about="#mwt1273" id="mwCus"/> chronicles and the <span title="Japanese-language text" about="#mwt1274" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCuw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"lang","href":"./Template:Lang"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja-latn"},"2":{"wt":"[[Man&apos;yōshū]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man'yōshū" title="Man'yōshū">Man'yōshū</a></i></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_Japanese-language_text" about="#mwt1274" id="mwCu0"/> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Japanese_poetry_anthologies" title="List of Japanese poetry anthologies" id="mwCu4">poetry anthology</a>, all from the 8th century and written in Chinese characters.<sup about="#mwt1277" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-307" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-307"}}'><a href="#cite_note-307" id="mwCu8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCvA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCvE">[</span>306<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCvI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1280" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-308" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-308"}}'><a href="#cite_note-308" id="mwCvM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCvQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCvU">[</span>307<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCvY">]</span></span></a></sup> In the early Heian period, the system of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phonogram_(linguistics)" title="Phonogram (linguistics)" id="mwCvc">phonograms</a> known as kana (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiragana" title="Hiragana" id="mwCvg">hiragana</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katakana" title="Katakana" id="mwCvk">katakana</a>) was developed.<sup about="#mwt1283" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-309" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-309"}}'><a href="#cite_note-309" id="mwCvo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCvs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCvw">[</span>308<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCv0">]</span></span></a></sup> <i id="mwCv4"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Tale_of_the_Bamboo_Cutter" title="The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter" id="mwCv8">The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter</a></i> is considered the oldest extant Japanese narrative.<sup about="#mwt1286" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-310" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-310"}}'><a href="#cite_note-310" id="mwCwA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCwE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCwI">[</span>309<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCwM">]</span></span></a></sup> An account of court life is given in <i id="mwCwQ"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Pillow_Book" title="The Pillow Book" id="mwCwU">The Pillow Book</a></i> by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sei_Shōnagon" title="Sei Shōnagon" id="mwCwY">Sei Shōnagon</a>, while <i id="mwCwc"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Tale_of_Genji" title="The Tale of Genji" id="mwCwg">The Tale of Genji</a></i> by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murasaki_Shikibu" title="Murasaki Shikibu" id="mwCwk">Murasaki Shikibu</a> is often described as the world's first novel.<sup about="#mwt1289" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-311" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-311"}}'><a href="#cite_note-311" id="mwCwo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCws"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCww">[</span>310<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCw0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1292" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-312" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-312"}}'><a href="#cite_note-312" id="mwCw4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCw8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCxA">[</span>311<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCxE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCxI">During the Edo period, the <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1293" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCxM" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[chōnin]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chōnin" title="Chōnin">chōnin</a></i></span> (<span class="gloss-quot" about="#mwt1294" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCxQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"gloss","href":"./Template:Gloss"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"townspeople"}},"i":0}}]}'>'</span><span class="gloss-text" about="#mwt1294">townspeople</span><span class="gloss-quot" about="#mwt1294" id="mwCxU">'</span>) overtook the samurai aristocracy as producers and consumers of literature. The popularity of the works of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saikaku" title="Saikaku" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCxY">Saikaku</a>, for example, reveals this change in readership and authorship, while <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bashō" title="Bashō" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCxc">Bashō</a> revivified the poetic tradition of the <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1295" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCxg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[Kokinshū]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kokinshū" title="Kokinshū" class="mw-redirect">Kokinshū</a></i></span> with his <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1296" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCxk" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[haikai]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haikai" title="Haikai">haikai</a></i></span> (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haiku" title="Haiku" id="mwCxo">haiku</a>) and wrote the poetic travelogue <i id="mwCxs"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oku_no_Hosomichi" title="Oku no Hosomichi" id="mwCxw">Oku no Hosomichi</a></i>.<sup about="#mwt1299" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-313" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-313"}}'><a href="#cite_note-313" id="mwCx0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCx4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCx8">[</span>312<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCyA">]</span></span></a></sup> The Meiji era saw the decline of traditional literary forms as Japanese literature integrated Western influences. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natsume_Sōseki" title="Natsume Sōseki" id="mwCyE">Natsume Sōseki</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mori_Ōgai" title="Mori Ōgai" id="mwCyI">Mori Ōgai</a> were significant novelists in the early 20th century, followed by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ryūnosuke_Akutagawa" title="Ryūnosuke Akutagawa" id="mwCyM">Ryūnosuke Akutagawa</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jun'ichirō_Tanizaki" title="Jun'ichirō Tanizaki" id="mwCyQ">Jun'ichirō Tanizaki</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kafū_Nagai" title="Kafū Nagai" id="mwCyU">Kafū Nagai</a> and, more recently, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haruki_Murakami" title="Haruki Murakami" id="mwCyY">Haruki Murakami</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenji_Nakagami" title="Kenji Nakagami" id="mwCyc">Kenji Nakagami</a>. Japan has two <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobel_Prize_in_Literature" title="Nobel Prize in Literature" id="mwCyg">Nobel Prize<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCyk">–</span>winning authors</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCyo">—</span><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yasunari_Kawabata" title="Yasunari Kawabata" id="mwCys">Yasunari Kawabata</a> (1968) and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenzaburō_Ōe" title="Kenzaburō Ōe" id="mwCyw">Kenzaburō Ōe</a> (1994).<sup about="#mwt1302" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-314" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-314"}}'><a href="#cite_note-314" id="mwCy0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCy4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCy8">[</span>313<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCzE">Japanese philosophy has historically been a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_fusion" title="Information fusion" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCzI">fusion</a> of both foreign, particularly <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_philosophy" title="Chinese philosophy" id="mwCzM">Chinese</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_philosophy" title="Western philosophy" id="mwCzQ">Western</a>, and uniquely Japanese elements. In its literary forms, Japanese philosophy began about fourteen centuries ago. Confucian ideals remain evident in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_society" title="Japanese society" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCzU">Japanese concept of society</a> and the self, and in the organization of the government and the structure of society.<sup about="#mwt1305" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Japanese_Confucian_Philosophy_315-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Japanese Confucian Philosophy"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Japanese_Confucian_Philosophy-315"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Japanese_Confucian_Philosophy-315" id="mwCzY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCzc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzg">[</span>314<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzk">]</span></span></a></sup> Buddhism has profoundly impacted Japanese psychology, metaphysics, and esthetics.<sup about="#mwt1308" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-316" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-316"}}'><a href="#cite_note-316" id="mwCzo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCzs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzw">[</span>315<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCz0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="31" id="mwCz4" aria-labelledby="Performing_arts"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Performing_arts">Performing arts</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1309" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCz8" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Music of Japan"},"2":{"wt":"Theatre of Japan"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1310" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1309" id="mwC0A">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Japan" title="Music of Japan">Music of Japan</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_of_Japan" title="Theatre of Japan">Theatre of Japan</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwC0E"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:春日神社ー篠山ー翁奉納P1011774.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwC0I"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:春日神社ー篠山ー翁奉納P1011774.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/%E6%98%A5%E6%97%A5%E7%A5%9E%E7%A4%BE%E3%83%BC%E7%AF%A0%E5%B1%B1%E3%83%BC%E7%BF%81%E5%A5%89%E7%B4%8DP1011774.jpg/250px-%E6%98%A5%E6%97%A5%E7%A5%9E%E7%A4%BE%E3%83%BC%E7%AF%A0%E5%B1%B1%E3%83%BC%E7%BF%81%E5%A5%89%E7%B4%8DP1011774.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/%E6%98%A5%E6%97%A5%E7%A5%9E%E7%A4%BE%E3%83%BC%E7%AF%A0%E5%B1%B1%E3%83%BC%E7%BF%81%E5%A5%89%E7%B4%8DP1011774.jpg/500px-%E6%98%A5%E6%97%A5%E7%A5%9E%E7%A4%BE%E3%83%BC%E7%AF%A0%E5%B1%B1%E3%83%BC%E7%BF%81%E5%A5%89%E7%B4%8DP1011774.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1280" data-file-height="960" data-file-type="bitmap" height="188" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwC0M"/></a><figcaption id="mwC0Q"><span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1311" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC0U" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[Noh]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noh" title="Noh">Noh</a></i></span> performance at a Shinto shrine</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwC0Y">Japanese music is eclectic and diverse. Many <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Traditional_Japanese_musical_instruments" title="Traditional Japanese musical instruments" id="mwC0c">instruments</a>, such as the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koto_(instrument)" title="Koto (instrument)" id="mwC0g">koto</a>, were introduced in the 9th and 10th centuries. The popular <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Japan#Folk_music" title="Music of Japan" id="mwC0k">folk music</a>, with the guitar-like <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1312" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC0o" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[shamisen]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shamisen" title="Shamisen">shamisen</a></i></span>, dates from the 16th century.<sup about="#mwt1317" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-317" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-317"}}'><a href="#cite_note-317" id="mwC0s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC0w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC00">[</span>316<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC04">]</span></span></a></sup> Western classical music, introduced in the late 19th century, forms an integral part of Japanese culture.<sup about="#mwt1320" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-318" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-318"}}'><a href="#cite_note-318" id="mwC08"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC1A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC1E">[</span>317<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC1I">]</span></span></a></sup> <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1313" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC1M" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[Kumi-daiko]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kumi-daiko" title="Kumi-daiko" class="mw-redirect">Kumi-daiko</a></i></span> <span class="gloss-quot" about="#mwt1314" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC1Q" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"gloss","href":"./Template:Gloss"},"params":{"mode":{"wt":"def"},"1":{"wt":"ensemble drumming"}},"i":0}}]}'>(</span><span class="gloss-text" about="#mwt1314">ensemble drumming</span><span class="gloss-quot" about="#mwt1314" id="mwC1U">)</span> was developed in post-war Japan and became very popular in North America.<sup about="#mwt1323" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-319" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-319"}}'><a href="#cite_note-319" id="mwC1Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC1c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC1g">[</span>318<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC1k">]</span></span></a></sup> Popular music in post-war Japan has been heavily influenced by American and European trends, which has led to the evolution of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J-pop" title="J-pop" id="mwC1o">J-pop</a>.<sup about="#mwt1326" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-320" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-320"}}'><a href="#cite_note-320" id="mwC1s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC1w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC10">[</span>319<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC14">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karaoke" title="Karaoke" id="mwC18">Karaoke</a> is a significant cultural activity.<sup about="#mwt1329" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-321" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-321"}}'><a href="#cite_note-321" id="mwC2A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC2E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC2I">[</span>320<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC2M">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwC2Q">The four traditional theaters from Japan are <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1330" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC2U" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[noh]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noh" title="Noh">noh</a></i></span>, <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1331" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC2Y" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[kyōgen]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyōgen" title="Kyōgen">kyōgen</a></i></span>, <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1332" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC2c" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[kabuki]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kabuki" title="Kabuki">kabuki</a></i></span>, and <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1333" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC2g" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[bunraku]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bunraku" title="Bunraku">bunraku</a></i></span>.<sup about="#mwt1337" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-322" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-322"}}'><a href="#cite_note-322" id="mwC2k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC2o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC2s">[</span>321<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC2w">]</span></span></a></sup> <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1334" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC20" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"Noh"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn">Noh</i></span> is one of the oldest continuous theater traditions in the world.<sup about="#mwt1340" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-323" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-323"}}'><a href="#cite_note-323" id="mwC24"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC28"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC3A">[</span>322<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC3E">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="32" id="mwC3I" aria-labelledby="Media"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Media">Media</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1341" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC3M" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Mass media in Japan"},"2":{"wt":"Censorship in Japan"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1342" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1341" id="mwC3Q">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mass_media_in_Japan" title="Mass media in Japan">Mass media in Japan</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Censorship_in_Japan" title="Censorship in Japan">Censorship in Japan</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwC3U"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Pokemon_Center_Tohoku.JPG" class="mw-file-description" id="mwC3Y"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Pokemon_Center_Tohoku.JPG" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Pokemon_Center_Tohoku.JPG/250px-Pokemon_Center_Tohoku.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Pokemon_Center_Tohoku.JPG/500px-Pokemon_Center_Tohoku.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2048" data-file-height="1152" data-file-type="bitmap" height="141" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwC3c"/></a><figcaption id="mwC3g">The Japanese media franchise <i id="mwC3k"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pokémon" title="Pokémon" id="mwC3o">Pokémon</a></i> is the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_highest-grossing_media_franchises" title="List of highest-grossing media franchises" id="mwC3s">highest-grossing media franchise</a> of all time.</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwC3w">According to the 2015 NHK survey on television viewing in Japan, 79% of Japanese watch television daily.<sup about="#mwt1346" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-324" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-324"}}'><a href="#cite_note-324" id="mwC30"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC34"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC38">[</span>323<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC4A">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_television_drama" title="Japanese television drama" id="mwC4E">Japanese television dramas</a> are viewed both within Japan and internationally.<sup about="#mwt1349" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-325" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-325"}}'><a href="#cite_note-325" id="mwC4I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC4M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC4Q">[</span>324<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC4U">]</span></span></a></sup> Many Japanese <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Media_franchise" title="Media franchise" id="mwC4Y">media franchises</a> have gained considerable global popularity and are among the world's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Highest-grossing_media_franchises" title="Highest-grossing media franchises" class="mw-redirect" id="mwC4c">highest-grossing media franchises</a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_newspapers" title="Japanese newspapers" id="mwC4g">Japanese newspapers</a> are among the most circulated in the world <span about="#mwt1343" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC4k" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"as of","href":"./Template:As_of"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2016"},"lc":{"wt":"y"}},"i":0}}]}'>as of 2016</span><sup class="plainlinks noexcerpt noprint asof-tag update" style="display:none;" about="#mwt1343"><a rel="mw:ExtLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Japan&amp;action=edit" class="external text" data-mw-original-href="//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Japan&amp;action=edit"><span typeof="mw:Entity">[</span>update<span typeof="mw:Entity">]</span></a></sup><span typeof="mw:Nowiki" about="#mwt1343"></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_potentially_dated_statements_from_2016" about="#mwt1343"/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:All_articles_containing_potentially_dated_statements" about="#mwt1343" id="mwC4o"/>.<sup about="#mwt1352" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-326" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-326"}}'><a href="#cite_note-326" id="mwC4s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC4w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC40">[</span>325<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC44">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwC48">Japan has one of the oldest and largest film industries globally.<sup about="#mwt1355" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-327" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-327"}}'><a href="#cite_note-327" id="mwC5A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC5E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5I">[</span>326<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5M">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ishirō_Honda" title="Ishirō Honda" id="mwC5Q">Ishirō Honda</a>'s <i id="mwC5U"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Godzilla_(1954_film)" title="Godzilla (1954 film)" id="mwC5Y">Godzilla</a></i> became an international icon of Japan and spawned an entire subgenre of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaiju" title="Kaiju" id="mwC5c">kaiju</a> films, as well as the longest-running film franchise in history.<sup about="#mwt1358" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-328" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-328"}}'><a href="#cite_note-328" id="mwC5g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC5k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5o">[</span>327<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5s">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1361" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-329" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-329"}}'><a href="#cite_note-329" id="mwC5w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC50"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC54">[</span>328<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC58">]</span></span></a></sup> Japanese comics, known as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manga" title="Manga" id="mwC6A">manga</a>, developed in the mid-20th century and have become popular <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manga_outside_Japan" title="Manga outside Japan" id="mwC6E">worldwide</a>.<sup about="#mwt1364" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Kinko_Ito_456–475_330-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Kinko Ito 456–475"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Kinko_Ito_456–475-330"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Kinko_Ito_456–475-330" id="mwC6I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC6M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6Q">[</span>329<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6U">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1367" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-331" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-331"}}'><a href="#cite_note-331" id="mwC6Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC6c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6g">[</span>330<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6k">]</span></span></a></sup> A large number of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_best-selling_manga" title="List of best-selling manga" id="mwC6o">manga series</a> have become some of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_best-selling_comic_series" title="List of best-selling comic series" id="mwC6s">best-selling comics series</a> of all time, rivalling the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_comic_book" title="American comic book" id="mwC6w">American comics industry</a>.<sup about="#mwt1370" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-332" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-332"}}'><a href="#cite_note-332" id="mwC60"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC64"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC68">[</span>331<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC7A">]</span></span></a></sup> Japanese animated films and television series, known as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anime" title="Anime" id="mwC7E">anime</a>, were largely influenced by Japanese manga and have become highly popular globally.<sup about="#mwt1373" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-333" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-333"}}'><a href="#cite_note-333" id="mwC7I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC7M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC7Q">[</span>332<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC7U">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1376" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-334" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-334"}}'><a href="#cite_note-334" id="mwC7Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC7c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC7g">[</span>333<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC7k">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwC7o">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Press_Freedom_Index" title="World Press Freedom Index" id="mwC7s">World Press Freedom Index</a> reported <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-censorship" title="Self-censorship" id="mwC7w">self-censorship</a> among journalists.<sup about="#mwt1379" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-s472_335-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"s472"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-s472-335"}}'><a href="#cite_note-s472-335" id="mwC70"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC74"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC78">[</span>334<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC8A">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="33" id="mwC8E" aria-labelledby="Holidays"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Holidays">Holidays</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1380" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC8I" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Public holidays in Japan"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1381" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1380" id="mwC8M">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_holidays_in_Japan" title="Public holidays in Japan">Public holidays in Japan</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1382" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC8Q" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"See also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Japanese festivals"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1383" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1382" id="mwC8U">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_festivals" title="Japanese festivals">Japanese festivals</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwC8Y"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Young_ladies_at_Harajuku.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwC8c"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Young_ladies_at_Harajuku.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Young_ladies_at_Harajuku.jpg/250px-Young_ladies_at_Harajuku.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Young_ladies_at_Harajuku.jpg/500px-Young_ladies_at_Harajuku.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2454" data-file-height="2367" data-file-type="bitmap" height="241" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwC8g"/></a><figcaption id="mwC8k">Young women celebrate <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coming_of_Age_Day" title="Coming of Age Day" about="#mwt1384" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC8o" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Nihongo","href":"./Template:Nihongo"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"[[Coming of Age Day]]"},"2":{"wt":"成人の日"},"3":{"wt":"Seijin no Hi"}},"i":0}}]}'>Coming of Age Day</a><span style="font-weight: normal" about="#mwt1384" id="mwC8s"> (<span title="Japanese-language text"><span lang="ja">成人の日</span></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_Japanese-language_text"/>, <span title="Hepburn transliteration"><i lang="ja-Latn">Seijin no Hi</i></span><span style="margin-left:.09em">)</span></span> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harajuku" title="Harajuku" id="mwC8w">Harajuku</a>, Tokyo.</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwC80">Officially, Japan has 16 national, government-recognized holidays. Public holidays in Japan are regulated by the <span about="#mwt1385" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC84" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Nihongo","href":"./Template:Nihongo"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Public Holiday Law"},"2":{"wt":"国民の祝日に関する法律"},"3":{"wt":"Kokumin no Shukujitsu ni Kansuru Hōritsu"}},"i":0}}]}'>Public Holiday Law</span><span style="font-weight: normal" about="#mwt1385" id="mwC88"> (<span title="Japanese-language text"><span lang="ja">国民の祝日に関する法律</span></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_Japanese-language_text"/>, <span title="Hepburn transliteration"><i lang="ja-Latn">Kokumin no Shukujitsu ni Kansuru Hōritsu</i></span>)</span> of 1948.<sup about="#mwt1388" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-336" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-336"}}'><a href="#cite_note-336" id="mwC9A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC9E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC9I">[</span>335<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC9M">]</span></span></a></sup> Beginning in 2000, Japan implemented the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happy_Monday_System" title="Happy Monday System" id="mwC9Q">Happy Monday System</a>, which moved a number of national holidays to Monday in order to obtain a long weekend.<sup about="#mwt1391" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-337" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-337"}}'><a href="#cite_note-337" id="mwC9U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC9Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC9c">[</span>336<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC9g">]</span></span></a></sup> The national holidays in Japan are <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_New_Year" title="Japanese New Year" id="mwC9k">New Year's Day</a> on January 1, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coming_of_Age_Day" title="Coming of Age Day" id="mwC9o">Coming of Age Day</a> on the second Monday of January, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Foundation_Day_(Japan)" title="National Foundation Day (Japan)" id="mwC9s">National Foundation Day</a> on February 11, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Emperor's_Birthday" title="The Emperor's Birthday" id="mwC9w">The Emperor's Birthday</a> on February 23, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vernal_Equinox_Day" title="Vernal Equinox Day" id="mwC90">Vernal Equinox Day</a> on March 20 or 21, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shōwa_Day" title="Shōwa Day" id="mwC94">Shōwa Day</a> on April 29, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitution_Memorial_Day" title="Constitution Memorial Day" id="mwC98">Constitution Memorial Day</a> on May 3, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenery_Day" title="Greenery Day" id="mwC-A">Greenery Day</a> on May 4, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Children's_Day_(Japan)" title="Children's Day (Japan)" id="mwC-E">Children's Day</a> on May 5, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marine_Day" title="Marine Day" id="mwC-I">Marine Day</a> on the third Monday of July, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain_Day" title="Mountain Day" id="mwC-M">Mountain Day</a> on August 11, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Respect_for_the_Aged_Day" title="Respect for the Aged Day" id="mwC-Q">Respect for the Aged Day</a> on the third Monday of September, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/September_equinox" title="September equinox" id="mwC-U">Autumnal Equinox</a> on September 23 or 24, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Health_and_Sports_Day" title="Health and Sports Day" class="mw-redirect" id="mwC-Y">Health and Sports Day</a> on the second Monday of October, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_Day" title="Culture Day" id="mwC-c">Culture Day</a> on November 3, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Labor_Thanksgiving_Day" title="Labor Thanksgiving Day" id="mwC-g">Labor Thanksgiving Day</a> on November 23.<sup about="#mwt1394" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-338" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-338"}}'><a href="#cite_note-338" id="mwC-k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC-o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC-s">[</span>337<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC-w">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="34" id="mwC-0" aria-labelledby="Cuisine"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Cuisine">Cuisine</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1395" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC-4" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Japanese cuisine"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1396" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1395" id="mwC-8">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_cuisine" title="Japanese cuisine">Japanese cuisine</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwC_A"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:East_West_sushi_01.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwC_E"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:East_West_sushi_01.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/East_West_sushi_01.jpg/250px-East_West_sushi_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/East_West_sushi_01.jpg/500px-East_West_sushi_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="6798" data-file-height="4492" data-file-type="bitmap" height="165" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwC_I"/></a><figcaption id="mwC_M">A plate of <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1397" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC_Q" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"[[nigirizushi]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nigirizushi" title="Nigirizushi" class="mw-redirect">nigirizushi</a></i></span></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwC_U">Japanese cuisine offers a vast array of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_regional_cuisine" title="Japanese regional cuisine" id="mwC_Y">regional specialties</a> that use traditional recipes and local ingredients.<sup about="#mwt1401" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-339" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-339"}}'><a href="#cite_note-339" id="mwC_c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC_g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC_k">[</span>338<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC_o">]</span></span></a></sup> Seafood and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_rice" title="Japanese rice" id="mwC_s">Japanese rice</a> or <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_noodles" title="Japanese noodles" id="mwC_w">noodles</a> are traditional staples.<sup about="#mwt1404" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-340" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-340"}}'><a href="#cite_note-340" id="mwC_0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC_4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC_8">[</span>339<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDAA">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_curry" title="Japanese curry" id="mwDAE">Japanese curry</a>, since its introduction to Japan from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Raj" title="British Raj" id="mwDAI">British India</a>, is so widely consumed that it can be termed a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_dish" title="National dish" id="mwDAM">national dish</a>, alongside <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramen" title="Ramen" id="mwDAQ">ramen</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sushi" title="Sushi" id="mwDAU">sushi</a>.<sup about="#mwt1407" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-341" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-341"}}'><a href="#cite_note-341" id="mwDAY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDAc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDAg">[</span>340<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDAk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1410" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-342" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-342"}}'><a href="#cite_note-342" id="mwDAo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDAs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDAw">[</span>341<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDA0">]</span></span></a></sup> Traditional Japanese sweets are known as <span title="Japanese-language romanization" about="#mwt1398" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwDA4" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"transliteration","href":"./Template:Transliteration"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"ja"},"2":{"wt":"wagashi"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="ja-Latn">wagashi</i></span>.<sup about="#mwt1413" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Goldstein2015_343-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Goldstein2015"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Goldstein2015-343"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Goldstein2015-343" id="mwDA8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDBA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDBE">[</span>342<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDBI">]</span></span></a></sup> Ingredients such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_bean_paste" title="Red bean paste" id="mwDBM">red bean paste</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mochi" title="Mochi" id="mwDBQ">mochi</a> are used. More modern-day tastes include <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_tea_ice_cream" title="Green tea ice cream" id="mwDBU">green tea ice cream</a>.<sup about="#mwt1416" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FujitaStallings2008_344-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"FujitaStallings2008"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FujitaStallings2008-344"}}'><a href="#cite_note-FujitaStallings2008-344" id="mwDBY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDBc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDBg">[</span>343<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDBk">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwDBo">Popular Japanese beverages include <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sake" title="Sake" id="mwDBs">sake</a>, a brewed rice beverage that typically contains 14–17% alcohol and is made by multiple fermentation of rice.<sup about="#mwt1419" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Batt2014_345-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Batt2014"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Batt2014-345"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Batt2014-345" id="mwDBw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDB0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDB4">[</span>344<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDB8">]</span></span></a></sup> Beer has been brewed in Japan since the late 17th century.<sup about="#mwt1422" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-BoultonQuain2013_346-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"BoultonQuain2013"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-BoultonQuain2013-346"}}'><a href="#cite_note-BoultonQuain2013-346" id="mwDCA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDCE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDCI">[</span>345<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDCM">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_tea" title="Green tea" id="mwDCQ">Green tea</a> is produced in Japan and prepared in forms such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matcha" title="Matcha" id="mwDCU">matcha</a>, used in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_tea_ceremony" title="Japanese tea ceremony" id="mwDCY">Japanese tea ceremony</a>.<sup about="#mwt1425" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-347" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-347"}}'><a href="#cite_note-347" id="mwDCc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDCg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDCk">[</span>346<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDCo">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="35" id="mwDCs" aria-labelledby="Sports"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Sports">Sports</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1426" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwDCw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Sport in Japan"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1427" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1426" id="mwDC0">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sport_in_Japan" title="Sport in Japan" class="mw-redirect">Sport in Japan</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwDC4"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Aki_basho_dohyō-iri_on_Sept._28_2014.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwDC8"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Aki_basho_dohyō-iri_on_Sept._28_2014.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Aki_basho_dohy%C5%8D-iri_on_Sept._28_2014.jpg/250px-Aki_basho_dohy%C5%8D-iri_on_Sept._28_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Aki_basho_dohy%C5%8D-iri_on_Sept._28_2014.jpg/500px-Aki_basho_dohy%C5%8D-iri_on_Sept._28_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="6016" data-file-height="4016" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwDDA"/></a><figcaption id="mwDDE"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sumo" title="Sumo" id="mwDDI">Sumo</a> wrestlers form around the referee during the ring-entering ceremony.</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwDDM">Traditionally, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sumo" title="Sumo" id="mwDDQ">sumo</a> is considered Japan's national sport.<sup about="#mwt1430" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-348" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-348"}}'><a href="#cite_note-348" id="mwDDU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDDY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDDc">[</span>347<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDDg">]</span></span></a></sup> Japanese martial arts such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judo" title="Judo" id="mwDDk">judo</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kendo" title="Kendo" id="mwDDo">kendo</a> are taught as part of the compulsory junior high school curriculum.<sup about="#mwt1433" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-349" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-349"}}'><a href="#cite_note-349" id="mwDDs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDDw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDD0">[</span>348<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDD4">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karate" title="Karate" id="mwDD8">Karate</a>, which originated in the Ryukyu Kingdom, is popular across the world and has been <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karate_at_the_Summer_Olympics" title="Karate at the Summer Olympics" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDEA">included in the Olympic Games</a>.<sup about="#mwt1436" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-350" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-350"}}'><a href="#cite_note-350" id="mwDEE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDEI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDEM">[</span>349<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDEQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1439" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-351" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-351"}}'><a href="#cite_note-351" id="mwDEU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDEY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDEc">[</span>350<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDEg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1442" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-352" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-352"}}'><a href="#cite_note-352" id="mwDEk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDEo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDEs">[</span>351<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDEw">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baseball_in_Japan" title="Baseball in Japan" id="mwDE0">Baseball</a> is the most popular sport in the country.<sup about="#mwt1445" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-353" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-353"}}'><a href="#cite_note-353" id="mwDE4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDE8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDFA">[</span>352<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDFE">]</span></span></a></sup> Japan's top professional league, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nippon_Professional_Baseball" title="Nippon Professional Baseball" id="mwDFI">Nippon Professional Baseball</a> (NPB), was established in 1936.<sup about="#mwt1448" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-354" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-354"}}'><a href="#cite_note-354" id="mwDFM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDFQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDFU">[</span>353<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDFY">]</span></span></a></sup> Since the establishment of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japan_Professional_Football_League" title="Japan Professional Football League" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDFc">Japan Professional Football League</a> (J.League) in 1992, association football gained a wide following.<sup about="#mwt1451" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-355" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-355"}}'><a href="#cite_note-355" id="mwDFg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDFk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDFo">[</span>354<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDFs">]</span></span></a></sup> The country co-hosted the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2002_FIFA_World_Cup" title="2002 FIFA World Cup" id="mwDFw">2002 FIFA World Cup</a> with South Korea.<sup about="#mwt1454" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-356" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-356"}}'><a href="#cite_note-356" id="mwDF0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDF4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDF8">[</span>355<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDGA">]</span></span></a></sup> Japan has one of the most successful football teams in Asia, winning the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asian_Cup" title="Asian Cup" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDGE">Asian Cup</a> four times,<sup about="#mwt1457" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-357" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-357"}}'><a href="#cite_note-357" id="mwDGI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDGM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDGQ">[</span>356<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDGU">]</span></span></a></sup> and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2011_FIFA_Women's_World_Cup" title="2011 FIFA Women's World Cup" id="mwDGY">FIFA Women's World Cup</a> in 2011.<sup about="#mwt1460" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-358" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-358"}}'><a href="#cite_note-358" id="mwDGc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDGg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDGk">[</span>357<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDGo">]</span></span></a></sup> Golf is also popular in Japan.<sup about="#mwt1463" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-359" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-359"}}'><a href="#cite_note-359" id="mwDGs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDGw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDG0">[</span>358<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDG4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwDG8">In <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motorsport" title="Motorsport" id="mwDHA">motorsport</a>, Japanese automotive manufacturers have been successful in multiple different categories, with titles and victories in series such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Formula_One" title="Formula One" id="mwDHE">Formula One</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Prix_motorcycle_racing" title="Grand Prix motorcycle racing" id="mwDHI">MotoGP</a>, and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Rally_Championship" title="World Rally Championship" id="mwDHM">World Rally Championship</a>.<sup about="#mwt1466" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-360" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-360"}}'><a href="#cite_note-360" id="mwDHQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDHU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDHY">[</span>359<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDHc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1469" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-361" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-361"}}'><a href="#cite_note-361" id="mwDHg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDHk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDHo">[</span>360<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDHs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1472" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-362" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-362"}}'><a href="#cite_note-362" id="mwDHw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDH0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDH4">[</span>361<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDH8">]</span></span></a></sup> Drivers from Japan have victories at the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indianapolis_500" title="Indianapolis 500" id="mwDIA">Indianapolis 500</a> and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/24_Hours_of_Le_Mans" title="24 Hours of Le Mans" id="mwDIE">24 Hours of Le Mans</a> as well as podium finishes in Formula One, in addition to success in domestic championships.<sup about="#mwt1475" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-363" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-363"}}'><a href="#cite_note-363" id="mwDII"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDIM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDIQ">[</span>362<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDIU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1478" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-364" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-364"}}'><a href="#cite_note-364" id="mwDIY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDIc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDIg">[</span>363<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDIk">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_GT" title="Super GT" id="mwDIo">Super GT</a> is the most popular national racing series in Japan, while <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Formula" title="Super Formula" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDIs">Super Formula</a> is the top-level domestic open-wheel series.<sup about="#mwt1481" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-365" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-365"}}'><a href="#cite_note-365" id="mwDIw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDI0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDI4">[</span>364<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDI8">]</span></span></a></sup> The country hosts major races such as the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_Grand_Prix" title="Japanese Grand Prix" id="mwDJA">Japanese Grand Prix</a>.<sup about="#mwt1484" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-366" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-366"}}'><a href="#cite_note-366" id="mwDJE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDJI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDJM">[</span>365<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDJQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwDJU">Japan hosted the Summer Olympics in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1964_Summer_Olympics" title="1964 Summer Olympics" id="mwDJY">Tokyo in 1964</a> and the Winter Olympics in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1972_Winter_Olympics" title="1972 Winter Olympics" id="mwDJc">Sapporo in 1972</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1998_Winter_Olympics" title="1998 Winter Olympics" id="mwDJg">Nagano in 1998</a>.<sup about="#mwt1487" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-367" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-367"}}'><a href="#cite_note-367" id="mwDJk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDJo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDJs">[</span>366<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDJw">]</span></span></a></sup> The country hosted the official <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2006_Basketball_World_Championship" title="2006 Basketball World Championship" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDJ0">2006 Basketball World Championship</a><sup about="#mwt1490" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-368" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-368"}}'><a href="#cite_note-368" id="mwDJ4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDJ8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDKA">[</span>367<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDKE">]</span></span></a></sup> and co-hosted the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_Basketball_World_Championship" title="2023 Basketball World Championship" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDKI">2023 Basketball World Championship</a>.<sup about="#mwt1493" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-369" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-369"}}'><a href="#cite_note-369" id="mwDKM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDKQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDKU">[</span>368<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDKY">]</span></span></a></sup> Tokyo hosted the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_Summer_Olympics" title="2020 Summer Olympics" id="mwDKc">2020 Summer Olympics</a> in 2021, making Tokyo the first Asian city to host the Olympics twice.<sup about="#mwt1496" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-370" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-370"}}'><a href="#cite_note-370" id="mwDKg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDKk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDKo">[</span>369<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDKs">]</span></span></a></sup> The country gained the hosting rights for the official <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Women's_Volleyball_World_Championship" title="Women's Volleyball World Championship" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDKw">Women's Volleyball World Championship</a> on five occasions, more than any other country.<sup about="#mwt1499" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-371" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-371"}}'><a href="#cite_note-371" id="mwDK0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDK4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDK8">[</span>370<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDLA">]</span></span></a></sup> Japan is the most successful Asian <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rugby_Union" title="Rugby Union" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDLE">Rugby Union</a> country<sup about="#mwt1502" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-372" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-372"}}'><a href="#cite_note-372" id="mwDLI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDLM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDLQ">[</span>371<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDLU">]</span></span></a></sup> and hosted the 2019 IRB <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rugby_World_Cup" title="Rugby World Cup" id="mwDLY">Rugby World Cup</a>.<sup about="#mwt1505" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-373" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-373"}}'><a href="#cite_note-373" id="mwDLc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDLg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDLk">[</span>372<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDLo">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="36" id="mwDLs" aria-labelledby="See_also"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="See_also">See also</h2></div>
